msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:7
#: 7c40e838be1240f3be456943ee00d0db
msgid "This article provides an overview of the fundamental principles of antenna design. Antenna design is an important factor in using UAVs over extended range and where there are obstructed views. While this is not directly related to Antenna Tracking/autopilots, it may be useful for some readers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:20
#: 9041f0b1be194d07b3f90dacc3ae57e5
msgid "Antenna fundamentals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:22
#: 80b5925c43324f1aa17ac578a69ccb8d
msgid "When we speak of antenna radiation it also includes the reverse, that is, the antenna 'collecting' radiated energy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:25
#: 80854099bdf546b9aed1ca9c578a67a7
msgid "Any piece of wire will radiate energy when connected to a source of RF ( Your transmitter or receiver). How WELL it radiates that applied energy is dependent on only two factors:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:29
#: 972ebe843cd74fc8bce25563299fb1d0
msgid "That the antenna is resonant at the same frequency as the radio signal applied and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:31
#: c774bfb48c0443f38608ec8dc753d22d
msgid "That the feedpoint of the antenna is matched to the impedance of the attached transmitter energy source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:36
#: 9222f8cdc41641009cce99b2f3171b71
msgid "Maximum energy transfer between source and load ( the antenna)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:37
#: 9a778a3056204e2c881383149e937859
msgid "occurs ONLY when the load and source impedance's are equal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:42
#: b05dad0305e74d7493d0943f5eb117d6
msgid "How well the antenna radiates that energy in any or all directions, if the above conditions are met, is then only dependent on the antenna design, shape, or style. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:46
#: 599a6bba2aad43159fed9ec9eac44edd
msgid "A simple vertical 1/4 wave radiator will radiate a doughnut shaped pattern, in all direction of the compass, with low energy upwards and downwards, in the direction of the antenna element."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:49
#: b260cb2bdf24495b979c71b7b5906d47
msgid "A yagi type antenna, on the other hand, ( such as your VHF or UHF TV antenna) focuses the energy in a single direction, as would a torch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:52
#: f77ca103266d4dae99dbba7dcb2b6a68
msgid "Antennas do not have gain:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:54
#: cffac35cf62c4f099066b21cf49966f5
msgid "They focus the energy to a greater or lesser extent, in a direction of design, but do this by robbing energy from other radiating directions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:57
#: e1dacf3d80354e959a1d22e78a15bc92
msgid "Your torch puts more light out the lens end, with nothing out the rear end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:59
#: e8c5fa2954ea4dbbb864b6544939bca0
msgid "If you remove the reflector from behind the torch bulb, the light is radiated in all directions, omnidirectionally, but is much weaker at any distant point than the focused beam."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:67
#: b4c4fdae041e46c69a7f4f444a79df38
msgid "Antenna radiation is polarized"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:69
#: 0db64abd50dd4d0b81cdcc31a9e2d200
msgid "That is to say, the radiated electromagnetic wave has a net polarization plane. This is usually either linear or circular (elliptical polarization is also found, but that is simply a mix of the two other types)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:74
#: 4beecfa855b94d51ab2230b01d7c15bc
msgid "A 1/4 wave vertical antenna will radiate linearly, with vertical polarization. Placed on its side it will radiate horizontal polarization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:77
#: df1091d09bc64b3ebb8ea0874c0db0c2
msgid "A Helical antenna (looks like a coil of wire wound in a screw fashion) wound clockwise when viewed from behind will radiate clockwise circular polarization, and vice versa."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:81
#: 6753921df8cc4cef83e7796aa6340958
msgid "For reception of maximum energy, the transmitting and receiving antennae must be identically polarized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:84
#: 9a3429298fd249fbbd4637454ec7fb3e
msgid "There is a massive loss of signal ( easily some 30dB, although the theoretical loss is infinite) if one attempts to receive a horizontally polarized signal with a vertically polarized antenna."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:87
#: 492f839e0bd54f5390467f6196df74e0
msgid "Similarly, there are massive losses if trying to receive a circularly polarized signal with an antenna of the opposite circular sense."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:89
#: fd42871ef95c4348bc85a8ced0c1e745
msgid "The odd man out is that there is only a 3db loss between an antenna that is circularly polarized and one that is linear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:92
#: 78002e9b54874bb8b1f2a581c012521a
msgid "You would use circular polarization if:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:94
#: d27eaca4c0324ca88ea074d2b0320b51
msgid "If the two antennas in question could not be made to maintain similar attitudes, such as one in a pitching , rolling aircraft, then there would be unacceptable signal losses as the aircraft banks and pitches."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:98
#: 26c3d25bebc54a408e72fc063eb377be
msgid "So you could use a vertical on the aircraft, and a helical or turnstile, or similar, on the ground segment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:100
#: a337092ceaee4422a1298cadfd903160
msgid "This way you would only ever experience a 3db maximum loss. ( all assuming good line of sight view)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:102
#: 27de7457dfd04a98a13e389571e9dd8e
msgid "Or you could gain back the 3db loss by fitting a similar circularly polarized antenna on the aircraft, giving the best of both worlds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:104
#: b416e83fb6354dfb8bc3ae8103d02904
msgid "But you actually gain more than that with circular polarization at both ends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:107
#: 58541a407ba44ca7bf6724845d7ecb98
msgid "Assume first that the two antennas are simple vertical monopoles, radiating vertically polarized signals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:110
#: 2609680c4abc4be1b10f3194392e9465
msgid "When you are flying, at the flying club, etc, you are probably near some metal structures, the 'hanger' , cars and other vehicles, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:112
#: 9c569a07cad4477d9c05dd4bf3716cb6
msgid "All these structures reflect the same energy you are trying to receive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:114
#: 5f1af6dc0fcf43899bdd0a43a53f45d3
msgid "In addition, when the aircraft is low and far, the RF transmitted by the A/C antenna follows two paths to your receiver - One directly, and one via a reflection from the ground, somewhat midway between you and the A/C."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:118
#: 9e0e0c0525bd40369572756a4c87ec42
msgid "What happens to the reflected wave is that the polarization is changed in unpredictable ways."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:120
#: 56de5f2f0b174f8c8d0b11b973efb6f5
msgid "Your receiver ( and antenna) does not know or care where the received energy comes from, so it receives this reflected energy as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:122
#: fe3cf192ca6d4058952599f88a9c6cad
msgid "These multitude of received waves add constructively and destructively with the main received wave, causing large, short duration, signal drop-outs - a sort of 'flutter' in the signal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:126
#: 72f8a5fb7a2a411a90aa8799e423bf0b
msgid "If both antenna are circularly polarized, however, the picture is quite different:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:129
#: 699fc37b734f486a963af85f2c78f662
msgid "When the circular polarized waveform is reflected , it REVERSES its polarization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:131
#: c61387df92fa4621a8f821ca5b19782d
msgid "When this reversed polarized signal arrives at your receiving antenna it is largely rejected and hugely attenuated, so interfering minimally with the main received signal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:142
#: 7ed66fe827fd452680c3f2d8956ec440
msgid "Resonance and matching issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:144
#: 6b9ab0e651e24cca89c24c7d6fa4a730
msgid "Most simple linear antenna are either of the monopole or dipole form."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:146
#: a500361393654c8a820eb7fcc5781250
msgid "A single monopole ( 1/4 wave vertical for example) or a single dipole will only radiate linear polarization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:149
#: 758d1c60e97743bb8a803f9a76a151c7
msgid "Any antenna is only resonant when it is exactly the correct length AT the frequency of operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:152
#: bc13e4ddd5c24cf5bacdc57ed519dc1a
msgid "( this does not apply to the class of broadband antenna, such as helical antenna, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:154
#: 6540f989019e4325b282d917ee29be7b
msgid "The helical will easily cover an ocatve with good performance)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:156
#: 687789d08dd6400fbe7446c2f86ed751
msgid "At resonance the antenna will exhibit its characteristic feedpoint impedance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:159
#: 2291332be853472f91ecd9b5e60daf5e
msgid "Feedpoint impedance is expressed with two terms, the pure resistive part, and the reactive ( j operator) part."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:162
#: d3905b69dee94d4abfe24d8c2fc530a8
msgid "Most transmitters and receivers terminal impedance are made to be 50ohms resistive, or very close to that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:165
#: 63006236a44d4b18b3a1168704dd7841
msgid "So it stands to reason the antenna must also be 50 Ohm resistive to have max energy transfer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:167
#: 4b8256a3a3e14308b596f95011aec266
msgid "However, none of the antenna are that obliging, so we have to do some feedpoint matching to meet the criteria."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:170
#: d3138858a64342a7881b6dcdfad85684
msgid "A 1/4wave vertical monopole over a ground plane has a resistive feed point of around 75ohms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:173
#: dbd351ab871b4b99b521312a188db124
msgid "A half wave dipole is around 72ohms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:174
#: d743c386197d48eeaf00b28d816e4f4d
msgid "As with resistors, placing two dipole in parallel as in the IBcrazy turnstile, will result in a feedpoint impedance of 35ohms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:177
#: dae6857a54d84ed59123ebb69552fdf3
msgid "A 75ohm feed connected to a 50ohm coax and transmitter will exhibit a 1.5:1 SWR ( the ratio of power going out to power reflected)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:180
#: 8e72f9ef0de24e0fb349ff9f3014831a
msgid "A 1.5:1 SWR means that approx 3% of your transmitter power is not being radiated: ( 30milliwatts for a 1watt transmitter)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:182
#: c151a831ab3a42afa21b08e76de10a3e
msgid "That is not so bad, and we can live with an SWR of 1.5:1 in most cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:185
#: 9b13734799464e16a4c28df5260e4852
msgid "The turnstile antenna is a pair of crossed dipoles, fed 90deg out of phase with each other, thereby generating circular polarisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:188
#: 7f894c04991d4f28a3d057897c0c4fa7
msgid "You CANNOT simply connect the dipole in parallel at the coax feedpoint though."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:190
#: ea26d6ea3dc84c03a69aa6d49f1e80b4
msgid "Apart from the halving of impedance ( which we decided we can live with) the radiation pattern and polarization of the antenna will be totally destroyed by unwanted radiation from the coax cable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:193
#: 043acc48a9a94e71a3c3293fe87d05be
msgid "The RF energy, at the dipole connection point, 'leaks' out and currents then flow down the outer shield of the coax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:195
#: de2130fdc85e4e9ead03b95668bb89a2
msgid "As mentioned previously, any piece of wire will radiate RF energy, and so the coax radiates this energy, and the radiation again adds constructively and destructively with the main antenna radiation, causes complete distortion and signal nulls in the pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:199
#: 66616ddc00b34eaf959748106bb59619
msgid "This radiation from the coax MUST be prevented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:201
#: fb6b9725fb59444b853590b2f2d4b3c6
msgid "This is done by means of a **Balun** transformer. - which is is an acronym for '**Bal**\\ anced to \\ **Un**\\ balanced transformer'."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:203
#: cc994a3328aa4f319df95a1ce236b9dd
msgid "A dipole is a balanced device - it is electrical equal along each element, outwards from the feedpoint. It therefore requires that the feedpoint be fed in a balanced fashion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:207
#: 53fdddd969a34c438183c53cd4df799b
msgid "Coax cable is a balanced feeder - the shield is at ground potential, while the inner core carries the energy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:209
#: 44726e4a3f7d4eb6a54dfbeb8822903f
msgid "This effectively ( oversimplifying a little) connects the one dipole half to the 'live' core, and the other half to 'ground' unbalancing the dipole."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:212
#: 2b82b856bf8944609e21c9bbb1182b63
msgid "This causes currents to flow on the coax outer shield, and distortion of the dipole radiation pattern."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:215
#: ccea11b37e234f619a5706b9984d4ad6
msgid "**Baluns** can be constructed from coax cable, but the accuracy required in coax cable length ( they are normally length multiples of 1/4 wavelength) is very critical, especially in the GHz range - 0.5mm can have a great effect."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-antenna-design.rst:219
#: 4841730d7d0c415199aaa23f86cf1407
msgid "The **turnstile** is not new - it is some 50 to 60 years old, and is well researched and published."
